          "turn_index": 1,
          "type": "turn",
          "question": "He is four.",
          "answer": "**If your son is having any of these signs now, get emergency help straight away:** fast breathing, skin pulling in between or under his ribs, grunting, nostrils flaring, or being too breathless to feed or talk. Call emergency services now. Also seek emergency care for blue or grey lips or face, or if he is very drowsy or hard to wake. These signs come from the \"Severe difficulty breathing\" guidance.\n\nBeing four does not change the warning signs above. The guidance I found on asthma diagnosis (from \"Asthma: confirming the diagnosis and the stepwise approach to controller therapy\") describes the symptom pattern and the breathing test, spirometry, but it does not say how these apply to children under five. Most children this young cannot do spirometry reliably, so the doctor will need to choose other ways to assess him. The material I searched does not explain those methods, so please ask his doctor how they will check his breathing.\n\nBecause he is four, it is a good idea to book a doctor's visit soon if the night wheezing keeps happening. Bring notes on when it happens, how often, and what seems to trigger it.\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
