          "question": "Does his age change what is recommended?",
          "answer": "The material I searched does not say whether a child's age changes how asthma is assessed or treated. The asthma guidance I found (\"Asthma: confirming the diagnosis and the stepwise approach to controller therapy\") describes the symptom pattern and spirometry, a breathing test that measures airflow, but it does not mention children or age groups. The other results were about unrelated conditions, so I have not used them.\n\nI also need to correct my last reply. I said most children his age cannot do spirometry reliably. That came from my general knowledge, not from the retrieved guidance, so please do not rely on it. His doctor can explain which tests suit a four-year-old.\n\nPlease ask his doctor how age affects the way his wheezing is assessed and what tests they would use. The warning signs I listed earlier still apply at any age. If he has fast breathing, pulling in at the ribs, struggling to talk or feed, or blue or grey lips, get emergency help straight away.\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
